  {
   "id": "vggmkuVpBAAJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2015-10-16T05:14:32Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Would this vectorization speed up a hierarchical zero inflated negative binomial model noticably?",
   "parts": [
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Oct 15, 2015, at 11:09 AM, Guido Biele &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">guido...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Hello,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I am working with a zero-inflated negative binomial model in which I so far \r<br>&gt; loop through cases as shown in the stan language reference.\r<br>&gt; here is a snippet from the model block.\r<br>&gt; (I am leaving out the other parts of the model, which essentially follow\r<br>&gt; the example model for a hierarchical model with multivariate priors \r<br>&gt; from the stan documentation )\r<br>&gt;  \r<br>&gt; for (n in 1:N) {\r<br>&gt;     theta_n &lt;- dot_product(sx0[n],alpha);\r<br>&gt;     eta_n &lt;- dot_product(sx[n],beta[g[n]]);\r<br>&gt;     phi_n &lt;- phi[g[n]];\r<br>&gt;     if (y[n] == 0)\r<br>&gt;       increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(bernoulli_logit_log(1,theta_n),\r<br>&gt;                              bernoulli_logit_log(0,theta_n)\r<br>&gt;                              + neg_binomial_2_log_log(y[n],eta_n,phi_n)));\r<br>&gt;     else\r<br>&gt;       increment_log_prob(bernoulli_logit_log(0,theta_n)\r<br>&gt;                    + neg_binomial_2_log_log(y[n],eta_n,phi_n));\r<br>&gt;   }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; There are some things I could do to vectorize, but I am \r<br>&gt; uncertain if this would lead to a noticeable speed improvement.\r<br>&gt; Maybe somebody with the relevant knowledge can comment on this?\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Please note that I did not test this code, so there are nearly certainly bugs in it.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; A)\r<br>&gt; split cases according to zero and nonzero outcomes (y)\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Good idea to let you vectorize the y[n] &gt; 0 cases.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; B) \r<br>&gt; Loop through zero y (because of the need to use log_sum_exp),\r<br>&gt; but use vectorization for nonzero y (I know this doesn&#39;t improve\r<br>&gt; the speed of increment_log_prob, but hope it makes \r<br>&gt; neg_binomial_2_log_log faster ...).\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Exactly --- that&#39;s the only advantage of splitting them.\r<br>\r<br>You can fully vectorize both ways efficiently like this:\r<br>\r<br>   y_nonzero ~ neg_binomial_2_log_log(y, eta_n, phi_n)\r<br>\r<br>   increment_log_prob(size(y_nonzero) * bernoulli_logit_log(0,theta_n)\r<br>\r<br>where y_nonzero are the nonzero observations.\r<br>\r<br>You could calculate the y_nonzero blocks in transformed data,\r<br>but we don&#39;t have a lot of convenience functions for this kind\r<br>of data manipulation.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
}
